  <bp:ProteinReference rdf:ID="ProteinReference5">
    <bp:organism rdf:resource="#BioSource1" />
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">UniProt:Q6P4A8 PLBD1</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PLBD1</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Lylap</bp:name>
    <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref34" />
    <bp:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION Lysosomal processive monoaminopeptidase that preferentially cleaves hydrophobic amino acids, with strong preference for leucine residues. Plays a key role in the degradation of hydrophobic transmembrane domains within the lysosome.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY an N-terminal L-leucyl-L-alpha-aminoacyl-[peptide] + H2O = an N-terminal L-alpha-aminoacyl-[peptide] + L-leucineSUBUNIT May form a homodimer, each monomer is composed of a chain A and a chain B.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION Expressed in neutrophils and monocytes.INDUCTION Up-regulated in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.PTM Proteolytically activated within lysosomes. Undergoes autocatalytic cleavage into an N-terminal A chain and a C-terminal B chain (PubMed:40146846). Both chains A and B remain associated in the mature protein (By similarity). An additional maturation step involves the removal of a segment spanning residues Leu-209-227-Cys, which occludes the active site (PubMed:40146846).SIMILARITY Belongs to the Ntn hydrolase family.CAUTION Was shown to exhibit a weak phospholipase activity, acting on various phospholipids, including phosphatidylcholine, phosphatidylinositol, phosphatidylethanolamine and lysophospholipids (in vitro) (PubMed:19019078). However, phospholipase activity is not confirmed by other authors (PubMed:40146846). Furthermore, structural feature prediction does not support the idea that LYLAP is a phospholipase (By similarity).</bp:comment>
  </bp:ProteinReference>
